The circumstances includes:
i) The High Court's decision is based on totally erroneous view of law by ignoring the settled legal position;
ii) The High Court's conclusions are contrary to evidence and documents on record;
Crl.A.No.104/09, dated 11.08.2010 KS,J crla_937_2013 iii) The entire approach of the High Court in dealing with the evidence was patently illegal leading to grave miscarriage of justice;
The Food Inspector Rep., By The Pp vs Meda Bapu Reddy on 1 October, 2024 Indian Kanoon - http://indiankanoon.org/doc/148970869/
2 iv)

The High Court's judgment is manifestly unjust and unreasonable based on erroneous law and facts on the record of the case;
v) This Court must always give proper weight and consideration to the findings of the High Court;
vi) This Court would be extremely reluctant in interfering with a case when both the Sessions Court and the High Court have recorded an order of acquittal."
